Give the collector a slot per block for a revocation handle, supplied from outside the pool.

The first step of putting this heap under Sublet, and deliberately the step that changes no
behaviour: the storage exists, gc_init clears it, and nothing reads it yet. If the suite moves at
this commit, the cause is the storage and not the discipline, which is the only reason to split
the two.

WHY ONE SLOT PER BLOCK. The measurement behind it is in the counters patch: over the upstream
suite this heap allocates 369,904 objects and collects 250,171 of them in 54 cycles, a mean of 34
survivors against 4,633 collected. The cheaper-looking design, one revocation of a handle senior
to the whole pool per cycle, would be 54 revocations instead of 250,171, and it does not work
here: revoking the senior handle kills every alias below it including the survivors', and those
aliases are held by the program inside other objects, in registers and on the stack. A non-moving
collector cannot hand replacements to pointers it does not own. So each object carries its own
handle, and the handle needs somewhere to live.

WHY NOT CARVED FROM THE HEAP, the way the finaliser and weakref tables are. The experiment
compares this arm against the same collector without the discipline. A table taken out of the pool
would leave the two arms with different amounts of memory to collect, and the comparison would
carry that difference for the rest of its life. The port hands the slots in from outside instead,
so the pool is byte for byte the same in both arms and the only thing that differs is what happens
to a pointer. The cost moves to the domain's data region, which declares its own size and can
afford it, and the cost is larger than a first estimate said. A block is
4 * MP_BYTES_PER_OBJ_WORD, and mp_uint_t here is uintptr_t at eight bytes rather than the width of
a capability, so a block is 32 bytes and not 64. A 384 KiB heap is 12,288 blocks, and a handle
apiece is 192 KiB: HALF the heap, beside it. Measured on the built image, mpy_gc_handles_storage
is 0x30000 bytes and the domain's storage grows from 852,480 to 1,049,120, leaving 564,224 bytes
of stack where there were 761,008.

Most of those slots are never used, because only a block that begins an object needs a handle and
an object averages 2.3 blocks. A denser table would need an index that does not exist, and raising
MICROPY_BYTES_PER_GC_BLOCK would halve the table at the price of internal fragmentation, which is
a POLICY change and this experiment's condition is that the policy does not move. So the table
stays dense, and its size is reported rather than engineered away.

The size check is an assertion rather than a raise. gc_init runs before there is an NLR handler,
so raising there would hang the domain instead of reporting, and the port carries the same
invariant as a static assertion where both sizes are compile-time constants.

--- a/py/gc.h
+++ b/py/gc.h
@@ -115,4 +115,26 @@
 const mpy_gc_stats_t *mpy_gc_stats(void);
 #endif
 
+// MPY_SUBLET: the heap under the discipline. Every object leaves this collector as an alias of
+// its own rather than as an offset into the one capability the whole heap occupies, and the
+// handle that revokes it lives in a slot the port supplies, one per block.
+//
+// WHY THE TABLE IS NOT CARVED FROM THE HEAP, the way the finaliser and weakref tables are: the
+// experiment compares this arm against the same collector without the discipline, and a table
+// taken out of the pool would leave the two arms with different amounts of memory to collect. The
+// port hands the slots in from outside, so the pool is byte for byte the same in both arms and the
+// only thing that differs is what happens to a pointer.
+#ifndef MPY_SUBLET
+#define MPY_SUBLET (0)
+#endif
+
+#if MPY_SUBLET
+#include "sublet.h"
+
+// One slot per block of the pool, supplied by the port. gc_init checks that it is large enough
+// and refuses rather than running with a table that ends before the pool does.
+extern sublet_cap *mpy_gc_handles;
+extern size_t mpy_gc_handles_len;
+#endif
+
 #endif // MICROPY_INCLUDED_PY_GC_H
--- a/py/gc.c
+++ b/py/gc.c
@@ -212,6 +212,16 @@
     area->gc_last_free_atb_index = 0;
     area->gc_last_used_block = 0;
 
+    #if MPY_SUBLET
+    // One slot per block, and the pool must not outrun it: a table that ends early would hand out
+    // an object whose handle belongs to another. This is an assert and not a raise because
+    // gc_init runs before there is an NLR handler to catch anything, so raising here would hang
+    // the domain instead of reporting. The port carries the same invariant as a static assertion,
+    // where both sizes are compile-time constants and the mistake cannot reach a build.
+    assert(mpy_gc_handles_len >= gc_pool_block_len);
+    memset(mpy_gc_handles, 0, gc_pool_block_len * sizeof(sublet_cap));
+    #endif
+
     #if MICROPY_GC_SPLIT_HEAP
     area->next = NULL;
 
